        "question": "Show that the Euclidean norm on $\\mathbb{R}^n$, defined by $\\|x\\| = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2}$, satisfies the triangle inequality $\\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$ for any $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.",
        "answerKey": "To prove the triangle inequality \\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|:\n1. Use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality: |x \\cdot y| \\le \\|x\\| \\|y\\|.\n2. Expand the squared norm of x + y:\n   \\|x + y\\|^2 = (x + y) \\cdot (x + y) = \\|x\\|^2 + 2(x \\cdot y) + \\|y\\|^2.\n3. Apply Cauchy-Schwarz:\n   \\|x + y\\|^2 \\le \\|x\\|^2 + 2\\|x\\|\\|y\\| + \\|y\\|^2 = (\\|x\\| + \\|y\\|)^2.\n4. Take the square root of both sides to get:\n   \\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|.\nThis completes the proof."
