        "answerKey": "1. Differentiability of f at a means there exists a linear transformation T: R^n -> R such that:\n   lim_{h->0} [f(a + h) - f(a) - T(h)] / \\|h\\| = 0.\n2. Choose h = t e_i, where e_i is the i-th standard basis vector and t -> 0.\n   Then \\|h\\| = |t|. The limit becomes:\n   lim_{t->0} [f(a + t e_i) - f(a) - T(t e_i)] / |t| = 0.\n3. Since T is linear, T(t e_i) = t T(e_i). Thus:\n   lim_{t->0} [ [f(a + t e_i) - f(a)] / t - T(e_i) ] = 0.\n4. This implies that the partial derivative \\partial f / \\partial x_i (a) = lim_{t->0} [f(a + t e_i) - f(a)]/t exists and equals T(e_i).\n5. The gradient vector is defined as \\nabla f(a) = (\\partial f/\\partial x_1, ..., \\partial f/\\partial x_n). Hence, T(h) = \\nabla f(a) \\cdot h."
