        "answerKey": "1. Let epsilon > 0. Since x^(k) -> L, there exists an integer N such that for all k >= N, \\|x^(k) - L\\| < epsilon.\n2. Note that for any coordinate j (1 <= j <= m):\n   |x_j^(k) - L_j| = sqrt((x_j^(k) - L_j)^2) \\le sqrt(\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i^(k) - L_i)^2) = \\|x^(k) - L\\|.\n3. Therefore, for all k >= N:\n   |x_j^(k) - L_j| < epsilon.\n4. By definition, this means the sequence of coordinates x_j^(k) converges to L_j as k -> infinity."
