        "question": "Define the Gamma function $\\Gamma(n)$ and Beta function $B(m, n)$. Prove the relationship: $B(m, n) = \\frac{\\Gamma(m)\\Gamma(n)}{\\Gamma(m+n)}$.",
        "answerKey": "1. Definitions: Gamma(n) = \\int_0^\\infty x^{n-1} e^{-x} dx; Beta(m,n) = \\int_0^1 x^{m-1} (1-x)^{n-1} dx.\n2. Express Gamma(m)Gamma(n) as product of integrals. Let x = u^2, y = v^2:\n   Gamma(m) = 2 \\int_0^\\infty u^{2m-1} e^{-u^2} du; Gamma(n) = 2 \\int_0^\\infty v^{2n-1} e^{-v^2} dv.\n3. Gamma(m)Gamma(n) = 4 \\int_0^\\infty \\int_0^\\infty u^{2m-1} v^{2n-1} e^{-(u^2+v^2)} du dv.\n4. Convert to polar coordinates (u = r cos(t), v = r sin(t)):\n   Integrand becomes: r^{2(m+n)-1} e^{-r^2} cos^{2m-1}(t) sin^{2n-1}(t) r dr dt.\n   = [ 2 \\int_0^\\infty r^{2(m+n)-1} e^{-r^2} dr ] * [ 2 \\int_0^{\\pi/2} cos^{2m-1}(t) sin^{2n-1}(t) dt ].\n5. Identify parts: First term is Gamma(m+n), Second term is Beta(m,n).\n6. Therefore, Gamma(m)Gamma(n) = Gamma(m+n) * Beta(m,n) => Beta(m,n) = [Gamma(m)Gamma(n)] / Gamma(m+n)."
