        "question": "Find the surface area of the part of the paraboloid $z = x^2 + y^2$ that lies under the plane $z = 4$ using double integration in polar coordinates.",
        "answerKey": "1. Surface Area formula: SA = \\iint_D sqrt(1 + (dz/dx)^2 + (dz/dy)^2) dA.\n2. Computations: dz/dx = 2x, dz/dy = 2y. Integrand = sqrt(1 + 4x^2 + 4y^2).\n3. Region D: boundary is z = 4 => x^2 + y^2 = 4 (circle of radius 2).\n4. Convert to polar coordinates: x^2 + y^2 = r^2, dA = r dr dtheta.\n   SA = \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\int_0^2 sqrt(1 + 4r^2) * r dr dtheta.\n5. Evaluate inner integral: Let u = 1 + 4r^2 => du = 8r dr => r dr = du/8.\n   \\int_0^2 sqrt(1 + 4r^2) r dr = (1/8) \\int_1^{17} u^{1/2} du = (1/8) * [2/3 u^{3/2}]_1^{17} = (1/12)(17^{3/2} - 1).\n6. Evaluate outer integral: \\int_0^{2\\pi} dtheta = 2pi.\n7. SA = (2pi/12)(17^{3/2} - 1) = (pi/6)(17\\sqrt{17} - 1)."
