        "question": "Define the gradient of a scalar field $\\phi(x, y, z)$ and prove that $\\nabla\\phi$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces of $\\phi$. Illustrate with the example $\\phi = x^2 + y^2 + z^2$.",
        "answerKey": "1. Definition: $\\nabla\\phi = \\frac{\\partial\\phi}{\\partial x}\\hat{i} + \\frac{\\partial\\phi}{\\partial y}\\hat{j} + \\frac{\\partial\\phi}{\\partial z}\\hat{k}$.\n2. Level surface: $\\phi = c$ (constant). Let $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ be any curve on this surface, then $\\phi(\\mathbf{r}(t)) = c$.\n3. Differentiating: $\\nabla\\phi \\cdot \\frac{d\\mathbf{r}}{dt} = 0$, showing $\\nabla\\phi$ is perpendicular to every tangent vector on the level surface, hence perpendicular to the surface.\n4. Example: $\\nabla(x^2+y^2+z^2) = 2x\\hat{i} + 2y\\hat{j} + 2z\\hat{k} = 2\\mathbf{r}$, which is radially outward, perpendicular to the spheres $x^2+y^2+z^2 = c$."
