        "question": "Evaluate $\\nabla^2(r^n)$ where $r = |\\mathbf{r}| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$. For what values of $n$ is $r^n$ harmonic (i.e., $\\nabla^2(r^n) = 0$)?",
        "answerKey": "1. Compute $\\nabla r = \\mathbf{r}/r = \\hat{r}$. Then $\\nabla(r^n) = nr^{n-1}\\nabla r = nr^{n-2}\\mathbf{r}$.\n2. Apply divergence: $\\nabla^2(r^n) = \\nabla\\cdot(nr^{n-2}\\mathbf{r}) = n[r^{n-2}(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{r}) + \\mathbf{r}\\cdot\\nabla(r^{n-2})]$.\n3. $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{r} = 3$, $\\nabla(r^{n-2}) = (n-2)r^{n-4}\\mathbf{r}$.\n4. So $\\nabla^2(r^n) = n[3r^{n-2} + (n-2)r^{n-4}|\\mathbf{r}|^2] = n[3r^{n-2} + (n-2)r^{n-2}] = n(n+1)r^{n-2}$.\n5. For $r \\neq 0$: $\\nabla^2(r^n) = 0 \\Rightarrow n(n+1) = 0 \\Rightarrow n = 0$ or $n = -1$. Thus $r^0 = 1$ (trivial) and $r^{-1} = 1/r$ (Newtonian potential) are harmonic."
