        "answerKey": "1. Test conservativity: Check if $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{F} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n   $\\partial_y(2xz) - \\partial_z(x^2) = 0-0 = 0$; $\\partial_z(2xy+z^2)-\\partial_x(2xz) = 2z-2z = 0$; $\\partial_x(x^2)-\\partial_y(2xy+z^2) = 2x-2x = 0$. So $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative.\n2. Find potential $\\phi$: $\\partial\\phi/\\partial x = 2xy+z^2 \\Rightarrow \\phi = x^2y + xz^2 + g(y,z)$.\n3. $\\partial\\phi/\\partial y = x^2 + \\partial g/\\partial y = x^2 \\Rightarrow g = h(z)$.\n4. $\\partial\\phi/\\partial z = 2xz + h'(z) = 2xz \\Rightarrow h = c$. So $\\phi = x^2y + xz^2$.\n5. Integral $= \\phi(1,2,3) - \\phi(0,0,0) = (1)(2) + (1)(9) - 0 = 11$."
