        "question": "State and prove the Parallel Axes Theorem for the Moment of Inertia of a rigid body.",
        "answerKey": "1. Statement: The moment of inertia $I$ of a body of mass $M$ about any axis is equal to the moment of inertia $I_G$ about a parallel axis passing through the center of gravity G plus the product of the mass and the square of the distance $d$ between the two parallel axes: $I = I_G + M d^2$.\n2. Proof: Set the origin at the CG G. Let the z-axis pass through G. The moment of inertia about this axis is: $I_G = \\sum m_i (x_i^2 + y_i^2)$.\n3. Let a parallel axis pass through a point $P(a, b, 0)$ at distance $d = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$. The coordinates of $m_i$ relative to the new axis are $(x_i - a)$ and $(y_i - b)$.\n4. The new moment of inertia is: $I = \\sum m_i ((x_i - a)^2 + (y_i - b)^2) = \\sum m_i (x_i^2 + y_i^2 - 2a x_i - 2b y_i + a^2 + b^2)$.\n5. Split the sum: $I = \\sum m_i (x_i^2 + y_i^2) - 2a\\sum m_i x_i - 2b\\sum m_i y_i + (a^2+b^2)\\sum m_i$.\n6. Since the origin is at the CG, $\\sum m_i x_i = 0$ and $\\sum m_i y_i = 0$. Also $\\sum m_i = M$.\n7. Therefore: $I = I_G + M d^2$."
